<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Goris</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Goris is a town in the Syunik province of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. It is known for the cave dwellings of Old Goris and for its town of stone houses laid out on a regular plan in the 1870s. In 1921 it was the seat of the anti-Bolshevik Republic of Mountainous Armenia led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Garegin Nzhdeh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goris.jpg" alt="Goris" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See above · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goris_new_mix_2021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Goris lies in the valley of the Goris, or Vararak, River, surrounded by the Zangezur Mountains, at an average elevation of 1,385 metres. It is 254 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> and 67 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapan">Kapan</a>, the provincial capital. The mountains to the south and east are known for medieval cave dwellings carved from the soft rock. The place has been called Kores and Gorayk, and one explanation derives the name from Indo-European prelanguage words for rock and to be, meaning a rocky place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been settled since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> inscription of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rusa-i">Rusa I</a> of Urartu, who reigned from 735 to 713 BCE, names Guria or Goriaya among 23 countries he conquered, and some scholars connect it with Goris. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the settlement, called Kores, stood on the left bank of the river and appears in Stepanos Orbelian's 13th-century History of the Province of Syunik. In the early 16th century the region became part of Safavid Persia, and in 1813 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p>The Russians founded the town in 1870 as the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zangezur">Zangezur</a> uezd, and the new town was completed in 1876 near old Kores to a plan by a German architect. After the fall of the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 1920, a congress at Tatev declared the Republic of Mountainous Armenia on 26 April 1921, with Goris as its de facto capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garegin-nzhdeh">Garegin Nzhdeh</a> as leader. It capitulated on 13 July 1921 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> operations, on Soviet promises to keep Zangezur in Soviet Armenia. Under Soviet rule the town was a regional centre, and its economy grew with hydroelectric plants and new industry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Goris is a town in Syunik Province and the seat of the Diocese of Syunik of the Armenian Apostolic Church. The Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Saint Gregory the Illuminator</a> was built between 1897 and 1904, and in February 1921 Nzhdeh was announced there as commander of the forces of the coming republic. The stone pyramids of Old Kores and the 4th-century basilica of Saint Hripsime stand in the old town. The archaeological museum opened in 1948, and a house museum of the writer Axel Bakunts has operated since 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Goris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Following the failed February 1921 uprising in Yerevan, the anti-Bolshevik forces retreated there and in April 1921 proclaimed an autonomous Syunik, renamed the Republic of Mountainous Armenia in June, with its seat at <b>Goris</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-mountainous-armenia">Republic of Mountainous Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
